  <data name="en_pano35_description"><![CDATA[<div>Welcome to Ushafa Pottery, established in 1991 by Mrs. Maryam Babangida under her Better Life for Rural Women Project. The village of Ushafa and the pottery has become a major tourist attraction in Abuja since the visit of the former president of United States of America, Mr Bill Clinton and his daughter in August 2000. In 2001 the pottery also played host to a league of Mayors from across the globe, indeed it can be said that the Pottery has shifted global tourist attraction to the village of Usahfa which is now called “Bill Clinton Village”.</div><div><br></div><div>Visit Ushafa Pottery, home of traditional pot making and See traditional African Pottery at its Finest, see local homesteads, interact and commune with the local people at the village square &amp; market.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano44_description"><![CDATA[This is the Production room of the Modern Wing of the Pottery, here you will come in contact with decorative wares, fresh from the potter’s wheel. The main method of production is hand ‘throwing’ on the potter’s wheels.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano37_description"><![CDATA[This is the Glazing Room, here dried wares are decorated and colored coated before they are taken to the kiln for firing, and this is done in order to fix the coloration, make it watertight, and give it a shiny appearance that is appealing.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano38_description"><![CDATA[The Ushafa Pottery Centre is surrounded by other Gbagyi communities. The primary aim of establishing the centre was to provide rural women the opportunity of acquiring skills in order to turn them into world-class potters and consequently make them self-reliant. Today, cooking and storage pots, cups, ornamental flower vases, beads, figurines, ceramic jewelry and candle stands are all made at the Pottery.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano41_description"><![CDATA[The pottery is made up of Local and Modern section. This is the local wing of the pottery, it consist of 12 typical Gwari Local huts with walls made from red earth, this section &nbsp;is manned by traditional gbagyi women who have meticulously preserved the pottery skills handed down to them by their ancestor.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano42_description"><![CDATA[This is the open Kiln, at this location you will see several clay pots that are stacked. one part of the stack are pots that are pre-heated before they can be taken to the stack that are ready for final heating.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano43_description"><![CDATA[Visit Ushafa Pottery, home of traditional pot making and See traditional African Pottery at its Finest, see local homesteads, interact and commune with the local people at the village square &amp; market.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano36_description"><![CDATA[The semi-Finished ware of the modern wing holds a large collection of wares that ready to be taken to Kiln for Glazing.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano40_description"><![CDATA[Here one will see the finished wares from the local section of the pottery, the wares found in this room are storage earthen pots, decorative flower vase, candle stands, tiny milling pots and so much more.]]></data>


